# FS-Skia-UI Template Framework Analysis

Date: 2026-05-14

This document analyzes how FS-Skia-UI can become an opinionated template framework for new F# / Elmish / Skia projects, with deterministic Speckit, build, test, and scaffolding processes.

The short version: this repository already contains most of the right constraints. The next step is to make those constraints executable and reusable: a `dotnet new` template for structure, a FAKE build graph for deterministic commands, a stronger Speckit preset for generated specs/plans/tasks, and a small set of project-local Speckit skills/extensions for UI-specific evidence.

## Current Project Shape

Observed local structure:

- `src/Lib`, `src/Layout`, `src/Charts` are packable F# libraries with matching `.fsi` files.
- `samples/*` are user-facing smoke and gallery hosts.
- `tests/*` include semantic tests, smoke tests, package tests, layout tests, parity tests, and chart tests.
- `scripts/*.fsx` already capture public-API preload and evidence workflows.
- `.specify/memory/constitution.md` already defines FSI-first, visibility-in-FSI, Elmish/MVU boundary, synthetic evidence disclosure, mandatory tests, and safe diagnostics.
- `.specify/presets/fsharp-opinionated` and `.specify/extensions/evidence` already prove this repo can override Speckit behavior and add gates.
- `specs/*/readiness` already models a useful evidence directory for transcripts, baselines, smoke output, and review artifacts.

This makes FS-Skia-UI less like a normal application template and more like a seed for a governed product family: every new project should inherit build graph, API boundaries, evidence obligations, test layering, samples, and Speckit behavior.

## Online Research Notes

Spec Kit supports exactly the customization mechanism needed here. Extensions add commands, integrations, quality gates, and new development phases, while presets override templates, commands, scripts, terminology, and organizational standards. Official docs also state that preset/template resolution is layered: project-local overrides, installed presets, installed extensions, then Spec Kit core. Sources: https://github.github.com/spec-kit/reference/extensions.html, https://github.github.com/spec-kit/reference/presets.html, https://github.com/github/spec-kit

Spec Kit's SDD model treats specs as the primary artifact and code as the expression of those specs. Its own description emphasizes constitution-driven constraints, research-driven context, generated plans, task derivation, parallelization, and traceable decisions. Source: https://github.com/github/spec-kit/blob/main/spec-driven.md

FAKE is a good fit for deterministic build orchestration because it is an F# build DSL with typed, declarative, extensible scripts, target dependencies, globbing, and module integration. Its own guidance says it is useful for reproducible/testable automation and for reducing CI-specific behavior, but should not replace MSBuild itself. Source: https://fake.build/guide/what-is-fake.html and https://fake.build/

FAKE has a bootstrap template that creates `build.fsx`, `fake.sh`, and `fake.cmd`, with local-tool bootstrapping and inline/file dependency modes. Source: https://fake.build/guide/fake-template.html

Elmish officially defines the core architectural boundary used by this repo: immutable `Model`, discriminated-union `Message`, `Command`, pure `init`, pure `update`, pure `view`, and a `Program` dispatch loop. It also emphasizes that the core does not depend on a particular UI technology and can run without a UI. Source: https://elmish.github.io/elmish/

SkiaSharp's API shape reinforces a clean rendering boundary: `SKCanvas` encapsulates drawing state, `SKPaint` holds style state, and the recommended raster/GPU path gets a canvas from an `SKSurface`. SkiaSharp also has multiple backends, including GPU surfaces, but does not create the graphics context for the host. Sources: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/skiasharp.skcanvas?view=skiasharp-3.119 and https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/skiasharp?view=skiasharp-3.119

Yoga is a strong choice for automatic layout because it is a portable, cross-platform, high-performance Flexbox layout engine, designed for speed, small size, and use outside web browsers. Source: https://chronesdev.github.io/Yoga/

`dotnet new` custom templates can replace a configured `sourceName` in file names and file contents, can be installed from a directory or package, and can be packed as a NuGet package. This is the right mechanism for deterministic project creation. Source: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/tools/custom-templates

Paket remains worth considering for template-family dependency determinism because a committed `paket.lock` records concrete direct and transitive dependency resolution and makes restore reliable across machines and time. Source: https://fsprojects.github.io/Paket/lock-file.html

Expecto is a good match for this repository's existing test style because tests are values, composable, async/parallel by default, and can be run from console apps or `dotnet` integration. Source: https://github.com/haf/expecto

## Recommended Template Boundary

The template should not try to regenerate the full project-specific feature history. It should freeze the framework shape and let specs drive feature growth.

Template-owned:

- Solution layout: `src/`, `tests/`, `samples/`, `scripts/`, `specs/`, `docs/`, `.specify/`, `.agents/skills/`.
- `Directory.Build.props` with `TargetFramework`, `LangVersion`, warnings-as-errors, package metadata placeholders, and FSI visibility enforcement.
- Canonical public API projects:
  - `src/Lib` for viewer/runtime/kernel API.
  - `src/Layout` for layout primitives and Yoga adapter boundary.
  - Optional `src/Charts` or `src/Widgets` depending on template flavor.
- Canonical test projects:
  - `tests/Lib.Tests`
  - `tests/Layout.Tests`
  - `tests/Smoke.Tests`
  - `tests/Package.Tests`
  - optional `tests/Parity.Tests`
- Canonical sample projects:
  - `samples/BasicViewer`
  - `samples/InteractiveViewer`
  - `samples/ScreenshotGallery`
  - optional feature galleries.
- Build and evidence scripts:
  - `build.fsx`
  - `fake.sh` / `fake.cmd`
  - `scripts/prelude.fsx`
  - `scripts/refresh-surface-baselines.fsx`
  - smoke scripts and capture scripts.
- Speckit constitution, templates, preset, evidence extension, and custom skills.

Project-owned after instantiation:

- Product feature specs under `specs/`.
- Actual product modules and widgets.
- Concrete branding/package metadata.
- Concrete platform support claims.
- Product-specific samples.
- Feature-specific readiness evidence.

Avoid putting generated product-specific feature specs into the template. Instead, include a `specs/000-template-profile/` or `docs/template-profile.md` that explains the inherited process.

## Framework Design Boundaries

The core design should make the rendering pipeline explicit:

```text
Model
  -> update Msg Model
  -> view Model dispatch
  -> declarative scene/layout tree
  -> layout evaluation
  -> render commands
  -> Skia host draws via SKCanvas/SKSurface
```

The key boundary is that `view` creates FS-Skia-UI scene data, not direct Skia calls. Direct `SKCanvas` use should be an edge implementation detail or a deliberate escape hatch with tests.

Recommended layers:

- **Application/MVU layer**: owns `Model`, `Msg`, `init`, `update`, subscriptions, and effects.
- **View declaration layer**: pure functions that turn `Model` into scene/layout/widget descriptions.
- **Layout layer**: pure or deterministic evaluation of logical bounds, Yoga interop hidden behind F# records/unions.
- **Render planning layer**: converts computed logical tree into render commands or draw operations.
- **Skia host layer**: owns GPU/Vulkan/window lifecycle, `SKSurface`, `SKCanvas`, native resources, screenshots, frame loop, and diagnostics.
- **Evidence layer**: FSI transcripts, surface baselines, smoke output, screenshots, and performance reports.

This boundary prevents two common AI failure modes:

- Recreating a mini UI framework inside a feature.
- Bypassing MVU and layout contracts by drawing directly in a sample or callback.

## Speckit Constitution Improvements

The existing constitution is strong. For template use, add principles that capture the assumptions now known to be stable across projects.

Recommended additions:

1. **Template Contract Is Source-Controlled**

   New projects inherit a versioned template profile. Any deviation from the template-owned layout, build graph, or evidence graph must be recorded in `docs/template-deviations.md`.

2. **View Functions Produce Declarative UI Data**

   `view` must return framework scene/layout/widget data. It must not perform I/O, mutate host state, allocate long-lived native resources, or draw directly to `SKCanvas`, except through a documented custom-render escape hatch.

3. **Skia Resources Live at the Host Edge**

   `SKSurface`, `SKCanvas`, Vulkan contexts, images, fonts, and native handles are owned/disposed by host or renderer modules. Domain, layout, widget, and MVU modules may describe resources but not own backend lifetimes.

4. **Layout Is Logical Before Physical**

   Layout operates in logical coordinates. Pixel snapping, DPI scaling, and backend-specific rounding happen at render and hit-test boundaries and must be deterministic.

5. **Build Graph Is Canonical**

   `build.fsx` targets are the authoritative local and CI workflow. CI may call FAKE targets but must not reimplement restore/build/test/pack/smoke sequencing in YAML.

6. **Template Drift Is a Defect**

   If a feature adds a new test category, public module type, sample pattern, readiness artifact, or build target, the corresponding Speckit templates/preset and FAKE build graph must be updated or an explicit deferral recorded.

7. **Golden Evidence Has Owners**

   Surface baselines, screenshots, FSI transcripts, performance files, and generated task graphs must be reproducible by named build targets. Hand-edited evidence is allowed only when explicitly marked.

8. **Dependency Policy Is Locked**

   Every dependency must have a pinning policy, owner, upgrade command, and evidence target. Preview dependencies must have preview-risk notes in package documentation.

These should be added to `.specify/memory/constitution.md` and propagated into `.specify/presets/fsharp-opinionated/templates/constitution-template.md`.

## Speckit Preset Improvements

Keep the existing `fsharp-opinionated` preset but split it conceptually into:

- `fsharp-library-opinionated`: FSI-first, package baselines, Expecto, scripts, docs.
- `elmish-skia-ui-opinionated`: MVU, view returns scene data, Skia host edge, samples, screenshots, layout evidence.
- `template-framework-governance`: template drift, FAKE build graph, scaffold verification.

If Spec Kit preset stacking is used, these can become separate presets with priorities. If this remains project-local, keep one preset but organize templates with those sections.

